       These functions are the reentrant  equivalents  of  the  functions  de‐
       scribed  in rraannddoomm(3).  They are suitable for use in multithreaded pro‐
       grams where each thread needs to obtain  an  independent,  reproducible
       sequence of random numbers.

       The rraannddoomm__rr() function is like rraannddoomm(3), except that instead of using
       state  information  maintained  in a global variable, it uses the state
       information in the argument pointed to by _b_u_f,  which  must  have  been
       previously  initialized  by iinniittssttaattee__rr().  The generated random number
       is returned in the argument _r_e_s_u_l_t.

       The ssrraannddoomm__rr() function is like ssrraannddoomm(3), except that it initializes
       the seed for the random number generator whose state is  maintained  in
       the  object pointed to by _b_u_f, which must have been previously initial‐
       ized by iinniittssttaattee__rr(), instead of the seed associated with  the  global
       state variable.

       The  iinniittssttaattee__rr()  function  is like iinniittssttaattee(3), except that it ini‐
       tializes the state in the object pointed to by _b_u_f,  rather  than  ini‐
       tializing the global state variable.  Before calling this function, the
       _b_u_f_._s_t_a_t_e  field  must be initialized to NULL.  The iinniittssttaattee__rr() func‐
       tion records a pointer to the _s_t_a_t_e_b_u_f argument  inside  the  structure
       pointed to by _b_u_f.  Thus, _s_t_a_t_e_b_u_f should not be deallocated so long as
       _b_u_f  is still in use.  (So, _s_t_a_t_e_b_u_f should typically be allocated as a
       static variable, or allocated on the heap using mmaalllloocc(3) or similar.)

       The sseettssttaattee__rr() function is like sseettssttaattee(3), except that it  modifies
       the  state  in  the object pointed to by _b_u_f, rather than modifying the
       global state variable.  _s_t_a_t_e must first have  been  initialized  using
       iinniittssttaattee__rr() or be the result of a previous call of sseettssttaattee__rr().

